- venue: Frontiers in Endocrinology
- citation count: 76
[method]
The paper used an in vivo animal model of high-fat-diet-induced obesity with IL-6 knockout to examine inflammatory effects on bone. Based on the available section headings, the related methods included analysis of bone microstructure, histological analysis, RNA extraction/cDNA synthesis/qRT-PCR, and Western blot analysis. The inputs were bone and bone marrow samples from experimental animals in the HFD-induced obesity model, and the assays evaluated trabecular bone loss, osteogenesis, adipogenesis, senescence, and IL-6/STAT3 pathway activity. This relates to the proposed cross-sectional study because both address inflammation and bone quality in obesity, but the paper is not a cross-sectional postmenopausal human study and does not directly measure Wnt canonical pathway components, serum cytokines, or mechanical bone strength as specified in the research plan.
[results]
The supplied paper text does not provide numerical values, effect sizes, or statistical significance. The results section headings indicate that IL-6 knockout restrained trabecular bone loss in HFD-induced obesity, rescued decreased osteogenesis, attenuated adipogenesis of bone marrow, attenuated senescence of bone marrow cells, and suggested that IL-6 may accelerate senescence of bone marrow stromal cells through the IL-6/STAT3 pathway.
[context]
The experimental context was a HFD-induced obesity model in experimental animals with IL-6 knockout, using bone microstructure, histology, gene expression, and protein expression analyses. The supplied text does not specify sample sizes, animal strains, diet duration, imaging hardware, PCR primers, antibodies, or statistical tests. This differs from the proposed plan, which enrolls 63 postmenopausal women with T2D and obesity, obesity with normal glucose metabolism, or normal weight; measures serum TNF-alpha, IL-6, and adiponectin; performs bone tissue RT-PCR for inflammatory and Wnt genes; uses microCT, compression testing, and DXA; and analyzes correlations among inflammation, Wnt signaling, and bone mechanical properties.
[end related work 1]

[start related work 2]
[metadata]
- in_text_citation: [(Bathina, and Armamento-Villareal, 2023-07)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/85bacb1bd87fa9810c86d7d641e931d84a67b9e0)
- venue: Frontiers in Endocrinology
- citation count: 25
[method]
The paper describes a human therapeutic approach that increases canonical Wnt/beta-catenin signaling by subcutaneous administration of an anti-sclerostin antibody, Romosozumab, in postmenopausal women with osteoporosis. It also notes that Wnt pathway modulators targeting obesity-associated regulators such as Sfrp1 and WIF-1 are being explored preclinically and clinically. This is related to the proposed cross-sectional study because both center on canonical Wnt signaling as a determinant of bone quality in postmenopausal women, while the proposed study additionally tests whether systemic and local inflammation in T2D and obesity is associated with Wnt gene expression, microarchitecture, and mechanical strength.
[results]
Romosozumab treatment reduced the risk of vertebral fractures and clinical fractures in postmenopausal osteoporosis women, indicating that pharmacologic enhancement of Wnt signaling can improve fracture outcomes. The excerpt does not provide numerical risk reductions, confidence intervals, p-values, or placebo comparisons. The Wnt-targeting strategies for Sfrp1 and WIF-1 are described as ongoing preclinical or clinical trials without reported outcomes.
[context]
The Romosozumab evidence comes from human postmenopausal osteoporosis patients receiving subcutaneous injections, not specifically from T2D or obesity cohorts. The paper states that canonical Wnt signaling is associated with bone formation and that Wnt signal transduction appears redundant in obesity and T2DM, providing a mechanistic rationale for the proposed plan's hypothesis that inflammation in T2D/obesity may suppress Wnt signaling and impair bone microarchitecture and mechanical properties.
[end related work 2]

