[method]
The paper used hip osteoarthritis subchondral bone and serum from normal-weight hip OA subjects. It profiled serum adipokines by Luminex, analyzed the molecular composition of type I collagen in subchondral bone, performed micro-CT scanning with reconstruction and analysis, conducted gene expression and pathway analyses, and examined beta-catenin localization by immunocytochemistry. These methods are related to the proposed recruitment of hip OA patients and the broader plan to assess systemic adipokines, Wnt/beta-catenin-related signaling, and bone tissue properties.
[results]
The provided excerpt does not include numerical values, sample sizes, or statistical tests. The reported outcome is that resistin pathologically altered the type I collagen composition of normal-weight hip OA subchondral bone. No quantitative results for T2D, obesity, inflammatory cytokine levels, Wnt gene expression, micro-CT parameters, or mechanical strength are available in the supplied text.
[context]
Context is limited to the section headings and result title: the study focused on normal-weight hip OA subchondral bone, serum adipokine profiling, type I collagen composition, micro-CT, gene expression/pathway analysis, and beta-catenin localization. It does not specify recruitment criteria, group definitions, instrument settings, or statistical methods in the provided excerpt.
