[metadata]
- in_text_citation: [(Kuo et al., 2020-07)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/4b53eb785380f61704623472ce53dc1d671335b0)
- venue: PLoS ONE
- citation count: 12
[method]
The paper used dual-energy X-ray absorptiometry (DXA) scanning to assess body composition and bone mineral content in adults with type 2 diabetes. DXA-derived variables included total lean mass, total bone mineral content, total fat mass, arm fat mass, leg fat mass, android fat mass, arm-to-android fat ratio, leg-to-android fat ratio, and whole-body fat percentage. DXA whole-body fat percentage was compared with the Jackson and Pollock 7-site skinfold equation using Spearman correlation, Lin's concordance correlation coefficient, and Bland-Altman analysis. This method is related to the proposed DXA assessment because it shows DXA can be used in a T2D and overweight/obese population to quantify body composition and bone mineral content, although the paper did not measure visceral adipose tissue or report bone mineral density.
[results]
In 59 adults with type 2 diabetes (36 females and 23 males), DXA showed that females had lower total lean mass (36.4 kg versus 50.7 kg, p<0.001) and lower total bone mineral content (1.93 kg versus 2.78 kg, p<0.001) than males, while total fat mass was similar (20.8 kg versus 21.0 kg, p=0.913). Females had higher DXA body fat percentage (36.5% versus 28.5%, p=0.009) and higher peripheral-to-android fat ratios (arm-to-android 0.99 versus 0.69, p<0.001; leg-to-android 2.35 versus 1.90, p=0.006). DXA body fat percentage correlated with the 7-site skinfold estimate in the total sample (Spearman r=0.837, p<0.001), females (r=0.672, p<0.001), and males (r=0.885, p<0.001), but absolute agreement was only fair in females (CCC=0.307, 95% CI 0.162-0.439) and moderate in males (CCC=0.561, 95% CI 0.380-0.700). The mean DXA minus skinfold bias was 8.14% overall, 8.74% in females, and 7.22% in males, with 95% limits of agreement of -0.98 to 17.27 overall, 0.02 to 17.45 in females, and -2.43 to 16.86 in males, indicating that the skinfold equation tended to underestimate DXA body fat percentage. DXA and skinfold methods showed significant high correlations for regional fatness in arms, legs, and the android region; the strongest correlations were in female legs (r=0.76) and male android region (r=0.89), while only the female leg-to-android fat ratio showed a moderate correlation between methods (r=0.49).
[context]
The study enrolled 59 adults aged 25-80 years with type 2 diabetes under stable control, including 23 males and 36 females, recruited from an endocrinology and metabolism outpatient clinic. Median age was about 67.6 years in females and 67.7 years in males, BMI was 25.0 kg/m2 in females and 26.1 kg/m2 in males, HbA1c was 7.2% in females and 7.1% in males, and median diabetes duration was 10 years in both groups. The population was not restricted to obesity or postmenopausal women. Exclusion criteria included pregnancy, acute cerebrovascular accident, myocardial infarction, heart failure, renal failure, hepatic failure, and psychiatric disease. The DXA scan was described as a standard scan, but no scanner model, region-specific BMD protocol, or visceral adipose tissue measurement was reported. Therefore, the paper supports the use of DXA for body composition and total bone mineral content in T2D adults, but it does not directly provide evidence for visceral adipose tissue quantification, BMD, or a postmenopausal female subgroup.
[end related work 1]

Processing method: Overall research design: We will conduct a cross-sectional study examining the relationship between inflammation, Wnt signaling, and bone quality in postmenopausal women.
Processing method: Systemic inflammation measurement: We will collect venous blood samples after an 8-hour fast on the day before surgery. Serum will be isolated and stored at -80°C for cytokine analysis. We will measure TNF-α and IL-6 using the ELLA microfluidic immunoassay system, and adiponectin using ELISA immunoassay.
Processing method: Cohort recruitment and grouping: We will enroll 63 postmenopausal women (age >65 years) undergoing hip replacement surgery for osteoarthritis. Participants will be divided into three groups: 19 women with T2D and obesity (target HbA1c ~6.8%; BMI ~30 kg/m²), 17 women with obesity but normal glucose metabolism (BMI ≥30 kg/m²), and 27 normal-weight controls (BMI <25 kg/m²).


Processing method: Local bone inflammation and Wnt gene expression: During hip replacement surgery, we will obtain femoral head specimens and collect trabecular bone samples. These will be washed in sterile PBS and stored at -80°C. We will extract total RNA using TRIzol and perform reverse transcription followed by real-time PCR analysis. Target genes will include inflammatory markers (TNF-α, IL-6, IL-8, IL-10, ADIPOQ, SFRP5) and Wnt signaling components (SOST, WNT10B, LEF-1, WNT5A, GSK3β), with β-actin as the housekeeping gene.
Processing method: Bone mechanical property assessment: We will then perform compression tests to determine mechanical parameters including Young's modulus, ultimate strength, and yield strength.
Processing method: Statistical testing of associations: Statistical analysis will involve group comparisons using appropriate tests for normally and non-normally distributed data. We will perform correlation analyses using Spearman correlation to examine relationships between inflammatory markers, Wnt signaling components, and bone mechanical properties.
Processing method: Body composition and BMD assessment: We will also conduct DXA scans to assess body composition, bone mineral density, and visceral adipose tissue measurements.
